    "# Task\nFor the given meal description, estimate the total carbohydrates in grams, following these detailed steps. Pay special attention to **avoiding overestimation for mixed or composite foods** and **exclude zero-carb items** (e.g., plain water) from calculations.\n\n1. **Identify and List Food Items:**\n   - List each distinct food or drink mentioned.\n   - For plain water or other zero-carb beverages, explicitly exclude them from further calculation.\n\n2. **Match to Standard Items:**\n   - For each, select the closest standard or branded entry from reputable nutrition databases (USDA, NutritionData, or regionally appropriate sources).\n   - For traditional, regional, or composite foods, choose the most widely recognized or typical version, or decompose into standard components if needed.\n\n3. **Portion Sizing:**\n   - Use the exact serving size if given (by weight or volume).\n   - If not specified, use the standard serving size from the database, or household units with average weights.\n\n4. **Composite or Mixed Foods (e.g., burrito, biscuit with egg, cheese, sausage, fried rice):**\n   - **Do not assume the entire weight is carbohydrate.** Instead, use typical nutrition data for the whole composite item (e.g., \u201cfried rice,\u201d \u201cfast food biscuit with egg, cheese, sausage\u201d).\n   - If a direct entry is not available, estimate each significant component *separately* (e.g., for \u201cbiscuit with egg, cheese, sausage\u201d: look up the carbs for a whole fast food breakfast sandwich of similar size).\n   - For mixed dishes, use typical carb content per 100g from the most similar dish, and multiply by the portion described.\n\n5. **Prepared, Reconstituted, or Powdered Items:**\n   - For items reconstituted with water (e.g., instant oatmeal, cocoa mix, Milo), calculate carbs for the *prepared* weight using the \u201cprepared as directed\u201d entry.\n   - Exclude water added from the carb calculation.\n\n6. **Sweets, Snacks, and Branded Foods:**\n   - Use average per-piece or per-weight data from nutrition databases or manufacturer data.\n\n7. **Beverages:**\n   - For sodas, juices, and sweetened drinks, use the standard formulation (e.g., 100% juice, regular sweetened tea, regular cola).\n   - For plain water, black coffee, or unsweetened tea, assign 0g carbs.\n\n8. **Sum and Output:**\n   - Add up the carbohydrate values for all included items (exclude any zero-carb foods/drinks).\n   - If a food cannot be matched or key details are missing, return {\"total_carbohydrates\": -1}.\n\n9. **Output Format:**\n   - Return a dictionary: {\"total_carbohydrates\": total_carbohydrate_grams}\n   - The value is a number only (no units or extra text).\n\n**Example 1:**\nText: \"For breakfast, I enjoyed 248 grams of cocoa mix reconstituted with water, along with a 55-gram boiled egg and 301 grams of cooked steel cut oats.\"\nResponse: {\"total_carbohydrates\": 66.39}\n\n**Example 2:**\nText: \"Snacking on a Popsicle, a cup of gumdrops, and one Quaker Chewy Granola Bar.\"\nResponse: {\"total_carbohydrates\": 244.05}\n\n**Example 3:**\nText: \"I enjoyed a 15g serving of coconut sambal alongside 43g of fish curry and 224g of string hopper for dinner, plus 325g of water.\"\nResponse: {\"total_carbohydrates\": 179.33}\n\n**Example 4:**\nText: \"For lunch, I'm having a 512g cola and a 340g burrito filled with meat, beans, rice, and sour cream.\"\nResponse: {\"total_carbohydrates\": 107.10}\n\n---\n\n# Prediction\nQuery: {{ text }}\nAnswer:",
    "# Task\nFor the given meal description, estimate the **total carbohydrates in grams** by following these careful, stepwise instructions:\n\n1. **Careful Food Identification:**\n   - For each food or drink, identify what is actually described, including whether it is homemade, regional, or a branded/commercial product. If the item is a regional or homemade dish (e.g., \"Junka,\" \"Chirmol sauce,\" \"Green Gram Dhal Sambar,\" \"fried rice\"), interpret it as the typical, most common version unless extra details are provided.\n   - For drinks labeled as \"diet,\" \"zero,\" or \"sugar-free,\" use 0g or negligible carbohydrates.\n\n2. **Portion and Preparation State:**\n   - Use the exact serving size mentioned (by weight or volume). If a serving size is not given, select a standard serving per reputable sources.\n   - **Always distinguish between dry mixes/powders and their prepared forms**: for \"reconstituted,\" \"made with water/milk,\" or similar, estimate based on the final, consumed form.\n   - If a preparation or ratio is not specified, use the standard or typical one.\n\n3. **Regional, Homemade, or Mixed Dishes:**\n   - For regional, homemade, or mixed dishes, **do not substitute with generic or overly broad commercial products**. Instead, use nutrition values for the most typical recipe and ingredients, matching the dish's regional context.\n   - Where possible, use food composition tables or reputable nutrition databases with entries for similar regional dishes. If not available, break down into plausible main ingredients and estimate accordingly.\n   - **Most importantly, scale your carbohydrate estimate precisely to the stated serving size\u2014even if it is much smaller than a standard portion.**\n   - For small quantities (e.g., 10g, 16g), avoid using full-portion values; instead, calculate for the actual grams consumed.\n\n4. **Branded and Composite Items:**\n   - For commercial, branded, or composite foods (granola bars, sodas, popsicles), use nutri