  "candidates": [
    "# Task\nFor the given meal description, compute the total carbohydrate amount in grams by following these detailed instructions. **Be mindful to avoid overestimating carbohydrates for mixed dishes and exclude any items with zero carbohydrate content (like plain water) from your calculations.** If you encounter unclear food items, branded products, or uncertain serving sizes, consult the fallback methods outlined below.\n\n**Step-by-step guidelines:**\n\n1. **Identify Food and Beverage Items:**\n   - List all distinct food or drink elements.\n   - Exclude water and other items with no carbohydrates (e.g., plain tea, black coffee).\n\n2. **Link Each Item to a Database Entry:**\n   - For every food or drink item, find the nearest match from reliable nutrition databases (USDA, NutritionData, or local equivalents).\n   - For branded or packaged products (e.g., \"small bag of Doritos,\" \"Goldfish crackers\"):\n     - Use standard serving sizes and nutritional data from the manufacturer or database for that specific item/brand.\n   - For traditional or mixed foods, choose the most widely recognized version or break it down into individual components if a direct match is unavailable.\n\n3. **Determine Portion Size:**\n   - If the quantity is given (in grams/ounces/ml/cups/pieces), utilize that number.\n   - For vague descriptions (e.g., \u201csmall bag,\u201d \u201cpersonal pizza\u201d):\n     - Use average weight/serving sizes from databases or packaging.\n     - For \u201cpersonal pizza,\u201d consider a standard personal (6-7 inch, ~150-200g) pizza entry.\n     - For \u201csmall bag,\u201d apply typical pre-packaged snack sizes (e.g., Doritos small bag = 28g, Goldfish small bag = 26g).\n     - For beverages, use standard volumes for cans/bottles/cups if unspecified.\n\n4. **Address Composite or Mixed Foods:**\n   - For complete items (e.g., \u201csteak and cheese sub with peppers and onions\u201d), prioritize database entries for the whole item (e.g., \u201csteak and cheese sub, with vegetables, fast food\u201d).\n   - If there\u2019s no direct entry, evaluate each main ingredient separately and total them.\n\n5. **Branded, Regional, and Unclear Foods:**\n   - If a specific branded entry isn\u2019t available, opt for the most similar generic equivalent.\n   - For regional cuisines, select the best match based on description and typical serving size.\n\n6. **Prepared, Mixed, or Reconstituted Beverages:**\n   - For reconstituted mixes (e.g., \u201cCyclone olive juice\u201d), apply the carbohydrate content for the total prepared weight as specified.\n   - Do not include added water in the carbohydrate total.\n\n7. **Sweets, Snacks, and Bakery Products:**\n   - Use the average carbohydrate content per piece, bag, or gram obtained from the database or manufacturer.\n\n8. **Calculate and Report:**\n   - Add up the carbohydrate values for all included foods and beverages.\n   - If an item cannot be identified or critical details are missing, return {\"total_carbohydrates\": -1}.\n\n9. **Output Format:**\n   - Provide the result in a dictionary format: {\"total_carbohydrates\": total_carbohydrate_grams}\n   - The value should be a numerical figure only (excluding units or extra text).\n\n**Example 1:**\nText: \"For breakfast, I enjoyed 248 grams of cocoa mix reconstituted with water, along with a 55-gram boiled egg and 301 grams of cooked steel cut oats.\"\nResponse: {\"total_carbohydrates\": 66.39}\n\n**Example 2:**\nText: \"Snacking on a Popsicle, a cup of gumdrops, and one Quaker Chewy Granola Bar.\"\nResponse: {\"total_carbohydrates\": 244.05}\n\n**Example 3:**\nText: \"I enjoyed a 15g serving of coconut sambal alongside 43g of fish curry and 224g of string hopper for dinner, plus 325g of water.\"\nResponse: {\"total_carbohydrates\": 179.33}\n\n**Example 4:**\nText: \"For lunch, I'm having a 512g cola and a 340g burrito filled with meat, beans, rice, and sour cream.\"\nResponse: {\"total_carbohydrates\": 107.10}\n\n**Example 5 (a